  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nAs surface high pressure over New York moves east, and ridging over\nthe southeastern states does the same, southerly flow over the Ohio\nValley will increase today. There is already considerable theta-e\nadvection occurring at 850mb-700mb ahead of a shortwave, with\nconvection ongoing in southern Indiana. This elevated convection will\nrepresent the first of several chances of storms over the next 24-48\nhours. Precipitable water values are already well over an inch and a\nhalf across the area, and some heavy rainfall will be possible as the\nstorms move into the Cincinnati tri-state region after 09Z.\n\nThe larger concern for heavy rain today will be with diurnally-driven\nstorms ahead of the approaching shortwave this afternoon.\nPrecipitable water values will be about 2 inches, if not slightly\nhigher, suggesting that storms will be capable of efficient rain\nrates. HREF PMM products suggest the potential for 2-4 inch rainfall\namounts in areas with training or slow-moving storms, though\ncertainly the exact placement of where this will occur is outside of\nthe scope of this prediction. The flood watch will remain in place as\npreviously issued, as the diurnal timing and greatest atmospheric\nmoisture content are most aligned with the eastern ILN CWA. Could not\ndiscount the possibility of a strong storm this afternoon, but thin\nCAPE profiles and marginal shear will preclude a larger threat.\n\nGoing into Sunday, attention will turn to a surface cold front, which\nis lagging well behind the shortwave moving in on Saturday. This\nfront will remain north of the ILN CWA until Sunday, which means that\ntemperatures will be able to get warm ahead of it, with highs in the\nlower to upper 80s. This also means that enough instability (around\n1500 J/kg SBCAPE) will build to produce some degree of a severe \nthreat. Shear is not especially impressive, but there is some\nreasonably favorable deep-layer westerly flow. Storm coverage is not\nlooking hugely impressive, though, with PoPs still in the chance\nrange.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nWith the frontal boundary well south of the region on Monday, and\nhigh pressure settling in over the Great Lakes, dry conditions are\nexpected for the ILN CWA on Monday. This will also be the coolest day\nof the period, with highs only in the mid to upper 70s.\n\nA larger shift to the weather pattern is expected to begin Tuesday,\nwith strong ridging (or even a closed high) setting up over the \nsoutheastern states. The main storm track and frontal zone will\nremain north of the ILN CWA to begin on Tuesday, but will shift\nsouthward as the ridge breaks down going into Wednesday and Thursday.\nAhead of this occurring, temperatures may get quite warm, with highs\nin the upper 80s to near 90 on Wednesday. Storm chances will increase\non Wednesday, then more significantly on Thursday, as the frontal\nzone drifts southward into the Ohio Valley.",
